      <h1>Heijōshin (平常心)</h1>
      <p class="concept-hero-subtitle">An important day does not require you to become someone else.</p>
    </div>
    <p class="news-meta">October 12, 2026</p>

    <p>
      During an ordinary class, you can accept a correction and return to the
      exercise. At a grading, the same mistake suddenly feels enormous.
      You begin wondering what the panel saw instead of attending to the partner
      in front of you. You hear the next instruction, but are still trying
      to repair the previous attempt in your head.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is an illustration, not an account of a particular student.
      It does not prove that the candidate is unprepared or insincere.
      Pressure can make familiar tasks harder. “Just don't be nervous” offers
      little guidance about what to do next. The useful question is how to
      return to work we know when the occasion matters.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Heijōshin</strong> offers a way to discuss that continuity.
      It does not promise freedom from nerves. It invites us to ask which habits
      of everyday practice are worth keeping on the day of a test, and how to
      practise them beforehand, without an audience.
    </p>

    <h2>Everyday composure and the Buddhist “ordinary mind”</h2>
    <p>
      <em>Digital Daijisen</em> describes 平常心, read <em>heijōshin</em>
      (へいじょうしん), as a mind unchanged from everyday life, a psychological
      state that is not unsettled. Jisho gives “self-possession” and
      “presence of mind”.<sup><a href="#heijoshin-source-1">[1]</a></sup><sup><a href="#heijoshin-source-2">[2]</a></sup>
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Nihon Kokugo Daijiten</em> also records <em>byōjōshin</em>
      (びょうじょうしん) in a Buddhist context: the ordinary, everyday mind,
      usual feelings and an ordinary way of living. It refers, among other texts,
      to Case Nineteen of the <em>Mumonkan</em>. These are different contexts,
      not two ingredients that must be combined to produce a mysterious state.<sup><a href="#heijoshin-source-1">[1]</a></sup>
    </p>
    <p>
      平常 concerns what is usual or everyday; 心 can mean heart or mind.
      We need not turn separate character glosses into a theory of practice.
      Here, “ordinary mind” is the starting point for a modern teaching interpretation:
      maintaining a familiar way of working even when we care deeply about the result.
    </p>

    <h2>“The ordinary mind is the Path” does not mean you already know everything</h2>
    <p>
      In Case Nineteen of <em>Wumen’s Gate</em>, known in Japanese as the
      <em>Mumonkan</em>, Zhaozhou asks Nanquan about the Path.
      J. C. Cleary translates the reply as
      “The ordinary mind is the Path.”<sup><a href="#heijoshin-source-3">[3]</a></sup>
    </p>
    <p>
      This is not a statement about passing a grading or performing an effective
      technique. It is a dialogue in the Chan tradition, known in Japanese as Zen,
      about the Path and awakening. When the questioner asks about moving toward it,
      Nanquan replies that doing so goes against it. Yet the conversation does not
      celebrate ignorance: not knowing is not offered as the answer either.
      In his commentary, Wumen adds that even after awakening, Zhaozhou still
      had thirty more years of study ahead.
    </p>
    <p>
      That context resists reducing the formula to “don't try” or “every everyday
      impulse is good”. Peter D. Hershock's account in the
      <em>Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</em> emphasises the situated nature
      of Chan teaching: statements respond to particular needs rather than always
      expressing final, universally valid formulas.<sup><a href="#heijoshin-source-4">[4]</a></sup>
    </p>
    <p>
      The inspiration I take for training is limited: you need not invent an
      extraordinary version of yourself to undertake a task. You do need something
      reliable to return to in practice. I am not treating the koan as an Aikido
      instruction or as a description of a psychological state produced by a
      specified number of repetitions.
    </p>

    <h2>After a mistake, hear the next instruction</h2>
    <p>
      Return to the candidate. Choosing the wrong side has already happened.
      Thinking about it during the next technique will not change it.
      You can hear the instruction, check that your partner is ready and begin
      the new task in the form you know. If the instruction is unclear,
      ask in accordance with the grading rules rather than guess and accelerate.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is not advice to ignore mistakes. Afterwards, review them and take
      the corrections seriously. During the current exercise, however, distinguish
      what you can still affect from what has passed. Dwelling on your performance
      can make it harder to notice what uke, the attacking partner who receives
      the technique, is doing now.
    </p>
    <p>
      Appearance is not a reliable verdict on composure. Louder breathing,
      trembling hands or stronger nerves do not prove that someone is “wearing
      a mask”. A careful bow is not evidence of pretence either.
      What matters more is understanding the task, responding to contact
      and respecting the limits of safe practice.
    </p>
    <p>
      Familiar habits may not be enough in every setting. A new partner,
      fatigue or different surroundings may require adaptation.
      <strong>Continuity does not mean copying a movement regardless of the situation.</strong>
      It includes slowing down, adjusting and stopping when safety requires it.
    </p>

    <h2>Preparation starts before you face the panel</h2>
    <p>
      Do not wait until grading day to practise responding to a mistake.
      In an ordinary class, a teacher can ask a student to show a short,
      familiar task in front of the group. The rules are clear, the partner
      knows the form, and the pace suits both people. Afterwards comes
      a specific correction, not a judgement of personality.
    </p>
    <p>
      You can also practise moving from one instruction to the next without
      rushing: finish the movement, remain attentive to your partner and check
      the conditions for the next repetition. This is not about surprising
      a student with an unannounced attack. Work under greater pressure still
      requires consent, preparation and the teacher's supervision.
    </p>
    <p>
      These are proposals to discuss in the dojo, not guaranteed ways to remove
      nerves. A brief rehearsal of the format may help one person; another may
      need more time or quieter conditions. A teacher should know the student
      well enough to distinguish a useful challenge from an unnecessary burden.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="/en/blog/exams-in-budo-showing-the-road-not-performing.html">Readiness for grading</a>
      still includes knowing the material, ukemi, partner work and applying
      corrections. “Do what you normally do” is not a way around the requirements.
      Composure does not guarantee a pass when the necessary skills
      have not been prepared.
    </p>
    <p>
      At a demonstration, the same principle means choosing material the pair
      can perform together and checking the conditions. We do not increase force
      or speed merely because visitors are watching. Responsibility for the
      person receiving the technique does not diminish as the audience grows.
    </p>

    <h2>Not a higher stage than fudōshin</h2>
    <p>
      Teaching can place different emphases on related terms.
      <a href="/en/blog/fudoshin-immovable-mind.html">Fudōshin</a> directs attention
      to steadiness under pressure. Heijōshin lets us ask about continuity
      in everyday practice. Their meanings overlap. They do not establish
      a strict boundary or a hierarchy in which one is more advanced.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="/en/blog/shoshin-beginners-mind.html">Shoshin</a> reminds us to remain
      open to learning. <a href="/en/blog/mushin-no-mind.html">Mushin</a> helps us
      discuss attention that does not get caught on one element. These are useful
      neighbours, but improving the next exercise does not require a chart
      of successive mental states.
    </p>
    <p>
      Likewise, our treatment of
      <a href="/en/blog/kakugo-enter-whole-or-not-at-all.html">kakugo</a> concerns
      readiness for difficulty and undertaking action responsibly.
      Such readiness can support practice on an important day. It does not
      switch off emotion or provide composure on demand. These distinctions
      are teaching tools, not exclusive definitions across every budō tradition.
    </p>

    <h2>Before an important conversation</h2>
    <p>
      Away from the mat, consider a presentation at work. You know the material,
      but are trying to sound like a much more confident person. You focus on
      the impression you make, then lose the thread at the first question.
      Instead of searching for the perfect role, you might return to your
      familiar way of explaining: listen, check what was unclear and answer
      one point at a time.
    </p>
    <p>
      Preparation still matters. Slides, rehearsal, notes and an agreed time
      limit are not a “mask”. They are tools for the task. Heijōshin need not
      mean abandoning support because you do not normally require it.
      Nor does it require staying in a harmful situation to prove composure.
    </p>
    <p>
      If anxiety persistently interferes with daily life, a philosophical phrase
      does not replace appropriate help. This example is an analogy, not a
      therapeutic promise. In training, we can practise something modest:
      return to the task after a mistake and notice the person we are working with.
    </p>
    <p>
      On the day of a test, you do not need to look like someone who never
      makes mistakes. <strong>Show the work you have prepared, not an idealised
      image of yourself.</strong> The next instruction is already being given.
      It is worth hearing.
    </p>

